From 45f96d697dd5a882f634df4a195d97887471f5db Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Neil Johnson <neil.johnson71@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 2 Sep 2026 17:46:14 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 11/26] asxxxx: do not clear the location counter's area on a
 '. = <arg>' error

Assembling a source containing a direct assignment of an absolute
value to the location counter,

	. = 4

segmentation faults every one of the 56 assemblers.  The minimal
reproducer is that one line.

equate() detects the error and reports it:

	sp = lookup(id);
	if (sp == &dot) {
		outall();
		if (e1->e_flag || e1->e_base.e_ap != dot.s_area)
			err('.');
	}

err('.') is not fatal, so the function runs on to record the area
the value belongs to:

	} else {
		sp->s_area = e1->e_base.e_ap;
	}

For an absolute expression there is no area and e_base.e_ap is
NULL, so this assigns NULL to dot.s_area - the very condition just
reported as an error.  At the end of the pass asmain.c:586 calls
newdot(dot.s_area) to flush the area, newdot() dereferences its
argument at asmain.c:3373, and the assembler dies:

    AddressSanitizer: SEGV on unknown address 0x000000000020
        #0 newdot asxxsrc/asmain.c:3373
        #1 main   asxxsrc/asmain.c:586

The location counter must always belong to an area;  main() sets
dot.s_area to &dca before each pass for exactly that reason.  Leave
it alone when the error fires.  The value assignment is unaffected,
the '.' error is still reported, and every other equate is
untouched:  the new branch is reached only when the symbol being
assigned is the location counter itself and the expression is
absolute.

This is not a corner case reachable only by a malformed file.
ascheck/equtst/equtst.asm is upstream's own test of assembler
equates;  it exercises 17 deliberate errors, each annotated in the
source with the code it should produce, and line 294 is

	. = 4			;.		program counter error

so the file has been unable to complete since that line was added.
Anything else containing the same construct fails the same way.

Verified:
- The one line reproducer now reports the documented error and
  exits 2 rather than 139:
    ?ASxxxx-Error-<.> in line 1 of min
                  <.> use ". = . + <arg>" not ". = <arg>"
- ascheck assembles equtst.asm to completion, producing all 17
  annotated errors and no others, and aslink links the result.
- Built every one of the 56 assemblers from these sources and from
  master's, and ran both over equtst.asm: all 56 crashed with 139
  before and all 56 exit 2 after.  The fault was in shared code and
  affected every target.
- Ran both builds of all 56 over asxtst/tconst.asm: byte identical
  .lst and .rel in every case, so nothing else moved.
- Built with -fsanitize=address,undefined and reran equtst.asm: no
  reports.
- Compiled with -Wall with no warnings.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 asxxsrc/asmain.c | 13 +++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 13 insertions(+)

diff --git a/asxxsrc/asmain.c b/asxxsrc/asmain.c
index cd93ffe..0e86f07 100644
--- a/asxxsrc/asmain.c
+++ b/asxxsrc/asmain.c
@@ -3115,6 +3115,19 @@ equate(char *id, struct expr *e1, a_uint equtype)
 	if (e1->e_flag && (e1->e_base.e_sp->s_type == S_NEW)) {
 		rerr();
 		xerr('x', "global argument NOT ALLOWED in an equate");
+	} else
+	if ((sp == &dot) && (e1->e_base.e_ap == NULL)) {
+		/*
+		 * A direct assignment of an absolute value to the
+		 * current location counter, '. = 4' rather than
+		 * '. = . + 4', has already been reported as a '.'
+		 * error above.  Do not go on to record the area of
+		 * the value, which for an absolute expression is
+		 * none:  the location counter must always have an
+		 * area, and newdot() dereferences it at the end of
+		 * the pass.
+		 */
+		;
 	} else {
 		sp->s_area = e1->e_base.e_ap;
 	}
